1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,769
A customer on version 1.4 reports
a crash on empty prompts.

2
00:00:08,809 --> 00:00:11,983
Support asks engineering:
is the fix in 1.4?

3
00:00:12,322 --> 00:00:16,969
Engineer one runs git branch --contains
with the ID of the fix from main.

4
00:00:17,009 --> 00:00:20,821
The release branch is not listed.
"It was never backported."

5
00:00:21,161 --> 00:00:24,460
Engineer two runs git cherry
between the release and main.

6
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:28,778
The fix is marked with a plus sign,
meaning "no equivalent on the release".

7
00:00:28,818 --> 00:00:30,390
"Confirmed, not backported."

8
00:00:30,730 --> 00:00:33,618
Engineer three opens the
file on the release branch.

9
00:00:33,658 --> 00:00:35,589
The two lines of the fix are there.

10
00:00:35,929 --> 00:00:40,422
Two tools said no and the file says yes.
All three observations are correct.

11
00:00:40,462 --> 00:00:45,194
By the end of this video you can explain
each of them, and name the one piece of

12
00:00:45,234 --> 00:00:48,415
evidence that would have
settled the question at once.

13
00:00:48,455 --> 00:00:51,874
Which engineer would you have
believed? Hold that thought.

14
00:00:53,714 --> 00:00:57,323
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:00:57,363 --> 00:01:02,287
The last two videos took cherry-pick apart.
A cherry-pick, remember, recomputes one

16
00:01:02,327 --> 00:01:07,132
commit's change on your current branch and
records it as a new commit, with a new

17
00:01:07,172 --> 00:01:11,678
ID. This video puts it to work, in the
job it's most used for: the backport.

18
00:01:12,018 --> 00:01:16,856
Three parts. First the workflow, four
steps, and the audit question that follows

19
00:01:16,896 --> 00:01:21,795
every backport: does the release branch
contain the fix, and how do you prove it?

20
00:01:21,835 --> 00:01:26,123
You'll ask that question in three
ways and get three different answers.

21
00:01:26,163 --> 00:01:30,879
Second, duplicate commits: after a pick,
the same change exists under two IDs.

22
00:01:31,219 --> 00:01:35,878
How do you find such pairs, and what
happens when the two branches are merged?

23
00:01:35,918 --> 00:01:40,758
Third, a short closing of a circle. Revert,
which undoes a commit by adding a new

24
00:01:40,798 --> 00:01:45,577
one, is the same operation as cherry-pick
with two inputs exchanged, and you can

25
00:01:45,617 --> 00:01:47,144
verify that with tree IDs.

26
00:01:47,484 --> 00:01:51,752
The project is still gateway,
with main and release/1.4.

27
00:01:53,592 --> 00:01:55,171
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:01:55,511 --> 00:01:56,923
After this video you can:

29
00:01:57,263 --> 00:02:00,313
Backport a fix to a release
branch and leave an audit trail.

30
00:02:00,653 --> 00:02:04,268
Establish in three ways whether
a release branch contains a fix.

31
00:02:04,608 --> 00:02:09,380
Find duplicate commits between two branches
with git cherry and --cherry-mark.

32
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:13,703
Explain what happens to duplicates when
the two branches are later merged.

33
00:02:14,043 --> 00:02:18,354
Relate revert to cherry-pick as the same
operation with base and theirs exchanged.

34
00:02:20,193 --> 00:02:24,960
The backport. In one sentence: a backport
is a fix that lands on the development

35
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,007
line first and is then copied to a maintenance
line with git cherry-pick -x, so that

36
00:02:30,047 --> 00:02:31,990
the copy says where it came from.

37
00:02:32,330 --> 00:02:34,862
Merge the fix into main
through the normal review.

38
00:02:35,202 --> 00:02:39,611
git switch release/1.4, then
git cherry-pick -x <commit>.

39
00:02:39,951 --> 00:02:44,659
Build and test on the release branch.
"Applied cleanly" says that the text merged,

40
00:02:44,699 --> 00:02:46,395
not that the code works there.

41
00:02:46,735 --> 00:02:51,556
Publish the release branch the way your team
publishes it: a push, or a pull request

42
00:02:51,596 --> 00:02:53,465
whose base is the release branch.

43
00:02:53,805 --> 00:02:58,502
Which direction? The textbook says a senior
engineer should be able to name both

44
00:02:58,542 --> 00:03:03,475
conventions. The Git project commits a fix
on the oldest supported branch that needs

45
00:03:03,515 --> 00:03:08,449
it and merges upward, so that newer branches
contain older ones and cherry-picks are

46
00:03:08,489 --> 00:03:09,277
the exception.

47
00:03:09,617 --> 00:03:14,215
Trunk-based practice fixes on the main
line first and cherry-picks to the release

48
00:03:14,255 --> 00:03:18,852
branch, on the argument that a fix made
on the release branch can be forgotten on

49
00:03:18,892 --> 00:03:22,975
main and come back as a regression.
This course demonstrates the second.

50
00:03:23,015 --> 00:03:24,978
The mechanics are the same in both.

51
00:03:25,318 --> 00:03:29,852
The audit. Three ways to ask
"is the fix in the release".

52
00:03:29,892 --> 00:03:35,950
On screen: the fix, f98ffd3, on main,
and its copy, c84bed2, on release/1.4.

53
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:41,361
By ancestry: git branch --contains
<original>. It follows parent links, and the

54
00:03:41,401 --> 00:03:45,048
original commit is not an
ancestor of the release branch.

55
00:03:45,088 --> 00:03:48,476
Its copy is. So this answers
"no" for every backport.

56
00:03:48,816 --> 00:03:53,140
By the recorded line: search commit messages
for "cherry picked from commit" and the

57
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:57,504
ID. If a commit on the release states that
it is a copy of the fix, that is a proof.

58
00:03:57,844 --> 00:04:03,082
By patch ID: git cherry. A patch ID is a
hash of a commit's diff with line numbers

59
00:04:03,122 --> 00:04:08,297
ignored. It pairs exact copies. It does
not pair a copy whose diff differs in any

60
00:04:08,337 --> 00:04:10,292
line, including a context line.

61
00:04:10,632 --> 00:04:16,188
Duplicates. In one sentence: after a
cherry-pick the same change exists under two

62
00:04:16,228 --> 00:04:21,783
commit IDs, and git cherry and git log
--cherry-mark find such pairs by comparing

63
00:04:21,823 --> 00:04:22,474
patch IDs.

64
00:04:22,814 --> 00:04:28,132
Precisely: git cherry <upstream> <head>
lists the commits of <head> that are not in

65
00:04:28,172 --> 00:04:33,554
<upstream> and marks each with a minus if
<upstream> contains a commit with the same

66
00:04:33,594 --> 00:04:38,976
patch ID, otherwise with a plus. git log
--left-right --cherry-mark A...B walks both

67
00:04:39,016 --> 00:04:44,205
sides of the symmetric difference, the
commits each side has and the other lacks,

68
00:04:44,245 --> 00:04:49,434
and marks such pairs with an equals sign.
--cherry-pick in place of --cherry-mark

69
00:04:49,474 --> 00:04:50,789
leaves the pairs out.

70
00:04:51,129 --> 00:04:55,650
Revert as the inverse. In one sentence:
git revert <commit> 🟡 CAUTION is the same

71
00:04:55,690 --> 00:05:00,323
three-way merge with base and "theirs"
exchanged: the base is the commit itself and

72
00:05:00,363 --> 00:05:03,644
"theirs" is its parent, so the
change is applied backwards.

73
00:05:03,984 --> 00:05:08,952
Read the two rows against each other: base
and theirs change places, and ours stays

74
00:05:08,992 --> 00:05:09,254
HEAD.

75
00:05:11,094 --> 00:05:14,813
Three witnesses, and each can
testify only to what it saw.

76
00:05:15,153 --> 00:05:20,158
Ancestry saw parent links. It can say "this
commit is reachable from that branch".

77
00:05:20,198 --> 00:05:22,065
It never saw a copy being made.

78
00:05:22,405 --> 00:05:27,302
The patch ID saw the text of a diff. It
can say "these two commits make textually

79
00:05:27,342 --> 00:05:32,118
the same change". It can't recognise the
same fix after it was adapted, or even

80
00:05:32,158 --> 00:05:34,373
after the surrounding lines differed.

81
00:05:34,713 --> 00:05:39,719
The message line written by -x is the only
witness that was told, at the moment of

82
00:05:39,759 --> 00:05:44,766
the pick, "this is a copy of that". It can
be wrong only if a person removed it or

83
00:05:44,806 --> 00:05:45,812
wrote it falsely.

84
00:05:46,152 --> 00:05:51,032
Where does this model break? None of the
three testifies that the code works on the

85
00:05:51,072 --> 00:05:55,300
release. That's step 3 of the procedure,
and no Git command replaces it.

86
00:05:57,140 --> 00:06:01,799
Read the picture from the top. Two commits
carry the fix, and no line of ancestry

87
00:06:01,839 --> 00:06:06,323
joins them. Each of the three audit
commands reads something different: parent

88
00:06:06,363 --> 00:06:08,412
links, the message, or the patch ID.

89
00:06:10,252 --> 00:06:13,609
Part 1: the backport. git
cherry-pick -x 🟡 CAUTION.

90
00:06:13,949 --> 00:06:19,613
--format=fuller shows both identities.
Asha wrote the change at 10:05, and you

91
00:06:19,653 --> 00:06:25,243
committed this copy at 10:09. The last
line of the message is the link to the

92
00:06:25,283 --> 00:06:28,680
original, with the full
ID that begins f98ffd3.

93
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:33,869
Part 2: three audits. The question:
is the fix in the 1.4 branch?

94
00:06:33,909 --> 00:06:38,532
First by commit ID. Predict what
git branch --contains prints.

95
00:06:38,572 --> 00:06:39,284
I'll wait.

96
00:06:44,124 --> 00:06:49,404
Only main. That's engineer one from the
opening. --contains follows ancestry, and

97
00:06:49,444 --> 00:06:53,411
the original commit is not an
ancestor of the release branch.

98
00:06:53,451 --> 00:06:54,199
Its copy is.

99
00:06:54,539 --> 00:06:56,363
Second, by the recorded line.

100
00:06:56,703 --> 00:07:03,616
git log --all --grep finds commit c308105,
and git branch --contains on that commit

101
00:07:03,656 --> 00:07:10,317
prints release/1.4. That is a proof: a
commit on the release states that it is a

102
00:07:10,357 --> 00:07:11,657
copy of f98ffd3.

103
00:07:11,997 --> 00:07:17,212
Third, by patch ID. Quick quiz, two options.
The mark in front of the fix: option

104
00:07:17,252 --> 00:07:20,975
one, a minus, meaning an
equivalent exists on the release.

105
00:07:21,015 --> 00:07:24,219
Option two, a plus, meaning
none. Say it out loud.

106
00:07:29,059 --> 00:07:32,319
Option two, a plus sign: "no
equivalent on the release".

107
00:07:32,359 --> 00:07:37,152
And here that's wrong. That's engineer two.
If you chose minus, your reasoning was

108
00:07:37,192 --> 00:07:40,393
sound, because the fix is
there. The tool can't see it.

109
00:07:40,733 --> 00:07:45,807
The release differs from main in a line
inside the hunk's context, the unchanged

110
00:07:45,847 --> 00:07:51,049
lines around the change: MAX_RETRIES = 3.
So the diff of the copy is not textually

111
00:07:51,089 --> 00:07:54,436
the diff of the original,
and their patch IDs differ.

112
00:07:54,476 --> 00:07:58,783
A context line was enough. Patch
comparison finds exact copies only.

113
00:07:58,823 --> 00:08:00,828
The -x line survives adaptation.

114
00:08:01,168 --> 00:08:07,415
Part 3: duplicates. labs/run ch10/duplicates.
A release branch whose own commit lies

115
00:08:07,455 --> 00:08:11,756
outside the context of the fix,
so that the copy is exact.

116
00:08:12,096 --> 00:08:16,555
The fix is f98ffd3 on main
and c84bed2 on the release.

117
00:08:16,895 --> 00:08:21,653
This time git cherry marks the fix with a
minus, in both directions: an equivalent

118
00:08:21,693 --> 00:08:23,115
exists on the other side.

119
00:08:23,455 --> 00:08:28,246
Read the markers. Less-than: a commit
only on the left side, the release.

120
00:08:28,286 --> 00:08:33,738
Greater-than: only on the right, main.
Equals: a commit whose change also exists on

121
00:08:33,778 --> 00:08:36,583
the other side. Two
equals lines, one pair.

122
00:08:36,923 --> 00:08:42,244
--cherry-pick leaves the pairs out, which
gives the real difference between the two

123
00:08:42,284 --> 00:08:47,153
lines. For "what is on main that the
release still lacks", add --right-only.

124
00:08:47,493 --> 00:08:48,679
The evidence underneath:

125
00:08:49,019 --> 00:08:52,910
The same patch ID in the first
column, two commit IDs in the second.

126
00:08:53,250 --> 00:08:58,248
Part 4: when the two lines meet again.
Merge the release branch back into main.

127
00:08:58,288 --> 00:09:03,478
Both sides contain the fix. Try it now, on
paper. Thirty seconds: write "conflict"

128
00:09:03,518 --> 00:09:08,516
or "clean", and how many times the fix's
subject appears in the log afterwards.

129
00:09:10,856 --> 00:09:15,944
Clean. Both sides made the identical change,
and identical changes don't conflict.

130
00:09:15,984 --> 00:09:21,134
That's a row of the three-way rule table.
The merge's stat shows only the release's

131
00:09:21,174 --> 00:09:21,822
own change.

132
00:09:25,962 --> 00:09:29,078
The history now holds the
change twice, once per ID.

133
00:09:29,118 --> 00:09:33,993
That's the third of this chapter's CTO
questions, "the log showed the same commit

134
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:38,666
title twice, did something go wrong?",
and the answer is: nothing went wrong.

135
00:09:39,006 --> 00:09:43,956
It becomes a problem when a copy was
adapted. Then the two sides changed the same

136
00:09:43,996 --> 00:09:49,131
lines differently, and the merge either
conflicts or, with unlucky context, combines

137
00:09:49,171 --> 00:09:50,980
them in a way nobody reviewed.

138
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:56,086
Part 5: revert, the inverse.
labs/run ch10/revert-inverse.

139
00:09:56,126 --> 00:09:59,898
The release has the
backported fix at its tip.

140
00:10:00,238 --> 00:10:05,174
Note the tree, the full snapshot of files,
of the commit before the backport: it

141
00:10:05,214 --> 00:10:10,088
begins c0427e5. Now predict the tree of
a revert with git merge-tree, using the

142
00:10:10,128 --> 00:10:14,940
inputs from the table: base is the commit
to undo, ours is HEAD, theirs is its

143
00:10:14,980 --> 00:10:15,375
parent.

144
00:10:15,715 --> 00:10:16,942
The same tree ID.

145
00:10:17,282 --> 00:10:21,066
The predicted tree, the tree of the revert
commit, and the tree of the commit before

146
00:10:21,106 --> 00:10:22,614
the backport are one and the same.

147
00:10:22,954 --> 00:10:27,245
A revert is a new commit like any
other. It uses the same sequencer, with

148
00:10:27,285 --> 00:10:32,228
REVERT_HEAD in place of CHERRY_PICK_HEAD
and the same four ways out, and it needs -m

149
00:10:32,268 --> 00:10:34,541
for a merge commit for the same reason.

150
00:10:40,581 --> 00:10:42,229
Five mistakes to watch for.

151
00:10:42,569 --> 00:10:46,845
Concluding "not backported" from
git branch --contains <original>.

152
00:10:46,885 --> 00:10:52,010
Root cause: ancestry follows parent links,
and a copy has no parent link to its

153
00:10:52,050 --> 00:10:52,598
original.

154
00:10:52,938 --> 00:10:56,590
Concluding "not backported"
from a plus sign in git cherry.

155
00:10:56,630 --> 00:11:01,783
Root cause: patch IDs pair textually identical
diffs; an adapted copy, or one whose

156
00:11:01,823 --> 00:11:04,160
context differs, has another patch ID.

157
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:09,146
Backporting without -x. Root cause: the
message line is the only record of the

158
00:11:09,186 --> 00:11:13,171
relationship that survives adaptation
and travels with the history.

159
00:11:13,511 --> 00:11:16,291
Treating "applied cleanly"
as "works on the release".

160
00:11:16,331 --> 00:11:20,548
Root cause: the merge compared text in
the files the commit touches; whether the

161
00:11:20,588 --> 00:11:23,421
release has what the fix
depends on was never checked.

162
00:11:23,761 --> 00:11:28,239
Generating a changelog from git log
across lines that exchanged picks.

163
00:11:28,279 --> 00:11:33,531
Root cause: each picked change is two
commits; anything that counts commits counts

164
00:11:33,571 --> 00:11:35,983
it twice unless --cherry-pick is used.

165
00:11:37,823 --> 00:11:42,823
Now, out of the lab. For an ML service
the maintenance line is often the version

166
00:11:42,863 --> 00:11:46,099
that a customer's evaluation
was signed off against.

167
00:11:46,139 --> 00:11:47,925
A backport changes that line.

168
00:11:48,265 --> 00:11:51,511
The textbook's advice: record
it where the sign-off lives.

169
00:11:51,551 --> 00:11:56,213
The -x line gives the auditor the original
commit, and the original commit leads to

170
00:11:56,253 --> 00:12:00,914
the review and the tests. That is a chain
from "what is running at the customer" to

171
00:12:00,954 --> 00:12:05,333
"who reviewed this change and what was
tested", and it exists only because one

172
00:12:05,373 --> 00:12:06,296
option was typed.

173
00:12:06,636 --> 00:12:11,555
The second point is for tooling. Anything
that counts commits double-counts

174
00:12:11,595 --> 00:12:16,713
duplicates: a changelog generated from
git log, a "commits since last release"

175
00:12:16,753 --> 00:12:22,135
metric. Generate such lists with --cherry-pick,
and remember its limit: an adapted

176
00:12:22,175 --> 00:12:27,160
backport is invisible to patch comparison.
Lab 10.3 works through that case.

177
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:32,474
So a release team's standing answer to
"is the fix in the release" has an order.

178
00:12:32,514 --> 00:12:36,861
First the -x line. Then patch
comparison, read with its limit in mind.

179
00:12:36,901 --> 00:12:40,684
And when both are silent, the
content of the file and a test.

180
00:12:40,724 --> 00:12:45,948
That first item is the evidence from the
opening: the -x line would have settled the

181
00:12:45,988 --> 00:12:47,013
question at once.

182
00:12:48,853 --> 00:12:53,816
Your turn. Do Lab 10.1,
"Backport a fix with -x", in

183
00:12:53,856 --> 00:12:57,375
lab-manual/m10-cherry-pick-ranges.md.

184
00:12:57,715 --> 00:12:59,544
Predict before each audit command:

185
00:12:59,884 --> 00:13:04,897
What will git branch --contains <original>
print after the backport, and why?

186
00:13:05,237 --> 00:13:07,757
What will a search for
the recorded line find?

187
00:13:08,097 --> 00:13:12,776
For git cherry: plus or minus in front
of the fix? Decide by looking at whether

188
00:13:12,816 --> 00:13:17,435
anything in the hunk, including its
context, differs between the two branches.

189
00:13:17,775 --> 00:13:22,063
And one prediction for step 3 of the
procedure: the pick applies cleanly.

190
00:13:22,103 --> 00:13:26,094
Is the release branch therefore
correct? Say how you would find out.

191
00:13:26,434 --> 00:13:32,884
The challenge is Exercise 10.10, Level 5,
""the fix is in the release" and "the fix

192
00:13:32,924 --> 00:13:37,575
was never backported"", in
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

193
00:13:39,415 --> 00:13:45,058
Q159: "A fix was backported with -x. Give
three ways to establish that the release

194
00:13:45,098 --> 00:13:50,255
branch contains it, and say which of
them can give a wrong answer and why."

195
00:13:52,595 --> 00:13:57,335
Answer out loud first. A strong answer names
three methods that read three different

196
00:13:57,375 --> 00:14:00,237
kinds of evidence, and for
each says what it reads.

197
00:14:00,277 --> 00:14:04,960
Then, for the "wrong answer" part, it's
specific about direction: which method says

198
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:09,740
"no" when the truth is "yes", under which
condition, and whether any of them can say

199
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:14,407
"yes" when the truth is "no". It closes
with the order in which you would use them

200
00:14:14,447 --> 00:14:18,845
during an incident, and what you check
when none of them gives a clear answer.

201
00:14:20,685 --> 00:14:24,813
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

202
00:14:25,153 --> 00:14:30,218
A backport is a cherry-pick onto a maintenance
branch with -x, followed by a build

203
00:14:30,258 --> 00:14:34,452
and test there, because a clean pick
says only that the text merged.

204
00:14:34,492 --> 00:14:39,122
Ancestry does not know about copies,
so --contains on the original says no.

205
00:14:39,162 --> 00:14:43,792
The recorded "cherry picked from" line
proves the relationship and survives

206
00:14:43,832 --> 00:14:44,477
adaptation.

207
00:14:44,817 --> 00:14:50,356
Patch IDs pair exact copies only, which is
what git cherry and --cherry-mark report,

208
00:14:50,396 --> 00:14:54,008
and --cherry-pick removes
such pairs from a comparison.

209
00:14:54,048 --> 00:14:59,321
Merging two lines that exchanged an exact
pick is clean and leaves the change in

